<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Di Xin</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 1046 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century-bce">11th century BCE</a> · Also known as King Zhou of Shang</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Di Xin was the last king of the Shang dynasty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/later-zhou">Later Zhou</a> tradition portrayed him as a cruel tyrant devoted to drink and his consort <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daji">Daji</a>, justifying his overthrow. In 1046 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/king-wu-of-zhou">King Wu of Zhou</a> defeated his army at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-muye">Battle of Muye</a>, and Di Xin died in his burning palace, ending Shang rule.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/di-xin.jpg" alt="Di Xin" width="320" height="452" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: 高井 伴寛、蹄斎 北馬 (Hiroshi Takai, Hossai Hokuma) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E7%B5%B5%E6%9C%AC%E4%B8%89%E5%9B%BD%E5%A6%96%E5%A9%A6%E4%BC%9D_%E6%AE%B7%E7%B4%82%E7%8E%8B.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
